          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"gissing-the-odd-women-51c21ceef8e19a00\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Everard directly avows his continuing love for Rhoda. The coordinated claim that he cannot abandon hope gives the avowal weak commitment-like pressure, since it signals persistence in his courtship, but the words “I love you” primarily present his emotional state as true. Nothing indicates that the words escape reflexively or function as affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The utterance’s conjunction with “I can’t abandon hope” weakly suggests an undertaking to persist, but continued courtship is largely contextual rather than a clear performative force of the love avowal itself.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Everard directly addresses Rhoda during their conversation, saying, “I love you, and I can’t abandon hope.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal answers Rhoda’s demand that their painful conversations end and effectively refuses her request that he withdraw. Everard’s subsequent confidence that she will eventually yield, regardless of whether their union is marriage or a free union, frames his declaration as possessive and persistent. That troubling relational stance bears on the use and reception of the utterance but does not alter its dominant truth-conditional force or create an independent O category.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you, and I can’t abandon hope.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person declaration strongly supports truth-conditional avowal; its second clause provides weak support for persistence or undertaking force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rhoda asks that they have spoken this way for the last time and that Everard not force her to avoid further “useless and painful conversations.”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration occurs as resistance to Rhoda’s attempt to end the courtship, explaining its persistent and pressuring contextual function.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After leaving, Everard remains sure that Rhoda “soon or late, would yield herself” and resolves that her life “should be linked with his.”\",\"supports\":\"His subsequent thoughts corroborate continued pursuit and possessiveness, while not showing that the love words themselves are reflex-like or a clear relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Earlier Everard says that because Rhoda is capable of her independent work, “I love you only the more.”\",\"supports\":\"The repeated, reason-giving presentation of love supports interpretation as a considered avowal rather than expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately represents the core force: a strong avowal with at most weak undertaking pressure from the attached refusal to abandon hope. Possessiveness and disregard of Rhoda’s boundary are contextual features rather than missing core-force dimensions.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
